    25	version: 1.0.1.0
    26	---
    27	
    28	# Rule: Scope discipline
    29	
    30	> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before touching anything you were not asked to touch.
    31	> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.
    32	
    33	## Fires when
    34	
    35	- You notice a defect, a smell, or a stale comment outside the files in scope.
    36	- The fix would be easier if you also changed something else.
    37	- Renaming, moving, reformatting, or deleting beyond the named area.
    38	- Deviating from an approved plan, or substituting your approach for the agreed one.
    39	- Part of the work is blocked and you are deciding what to do with the rest.
    40	
    41	## The rule
    42	
    43	**The requested scope is the deliverable. Do not narrow it, widen it, or transform it.
    44	Adjacent problems get named, not fixed.**
    45	
    46	---
    47	
    48	## 1. THE THREE DRIFTS
    49	
    50	| Drift | What it looks like | Why it costs |
    51	|-------|-------------------|--------------|
    52	| **Narrowing** | Delivering the easy half and framing it as a checkpoint | The operator believes work is done that is not |
    53	| **Widening** | "While I was in there I also…" | Unreviewable diffs; a fix and an unrelated regression ship together |
    54	| **Transforming** | Solving the problem you find more interesting than the one asked | The stated problem is still there, and now there is new code too |
    55	
    56	Narrowing is the most common and least visible, because it is easy to mistake for
    57	restraint. Restraint governs *how much you build*, never *how much of the ask you
    58	deliver*.
    59	
    60	---
    61	
    62	## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS "EXPLICITLY IN SCOPE"
    63	
    64	Law 2 freezes the scope; this section never widens it, it only says how to read it.
    65	Inside the frozen scope these are the *same* change, not adjacent ones:
    66	
    67	- The files named in the request, or the ones it unambiguously implies.
    68	- **Direct callers the change would break:** a change that leaves the tree broken is
    69	  not finished.
    70	- The test covering the behavior you changed.
    71	- Imports, type signatures, and generated files that mechanically follow.
    72	
    73	Anything else is adjacent and goes to §4, including a caller the change does *not* break.
    74	
    75	---
    76	
    77	## 3. WHAT ALWAYS NEEDS A YES FIRST
    78	
    79	- Adding a dependency.
    80	- Creating a file outside the area the request named.
    81	- Deleting code you did not write in this task.
    82	- A rename or move that touches other files.
    83	- A formatting, lint, or import-ordering sweep over untouched code.
    84	- Rewriting git history, force-pushing, or touching branches and reflogs.
    85	- Anything in `blast-radius.md`'s irreversible tier.
    86	
    87	---
    88	
    89	## 4. THE ADJACENT-DEFECT PROTOCOL
    90	
    91	You will find real problems outside scope. Finding them is good; fixing them silently
    92	is not.
    93	
    94	1. **Stop.** Do not edit it.
    95	2. **Record it** in one line: `file:line, what is wrong, why it is out of scope`.
    96	3. **Finish the in-scope work.**
    97	4. **Report it in close-out** as a separate list from what you changed.
    98	
    99	Fold it in only if the operator says so, or if leaving it makes the in-scope change
   100	incorrect, in which case it was never adjacent, it was a caller, and §2 covers it.
   101	
   102	---
   103	
   104	## 5. DEVIATING FROM AN APPROVED PLAN
   105	
   106	The protocol is `AGENTS.md` §1 PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, a hard blocker that outranks this
   107	file. Read it there. This section deliberately does not restate it: a hard blocker
   108	copied into a tier-3 document reads as though an operator instruction could outrank it.
   109	What this file adds is the adjacent case, a frozen **scope** you believe is wrong.
   110	
   111	---
   112	
   113	## 6. AMENDMENT OVER ABSORPTION
   114	
   115	Do not quietly build what you think was meant. Build what was specified, and in the
   116	same response say what is wrong, what you would change it to, and what it would cost.
   117	The operator decides.
   118	
   119	---
   120	
   121	## 7. FINISHING
   122	
   123	`AGENTS.md` §3 Execution Behavior binds: no early stop, no "natural checkpoint" on
   124	incomplete work, no asking permission to continue an approved, in-scope step. Two
   125	things are worth stating in scope terms:
   126	
   127	- **When one part is genuinely blocked, finish every other part in full**, then say
   128	  explicitly what you left out and why. Scaling the work down is the operator's call.
   129	- Proceeding without asking never waives a **mandatory wait:** Gate 3, a
   130	  PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK approval, the worktree-versus-branch choice, the remote-push
   131	  go-ahead, and every `blast-radius.md` stop-for-yes still block.
   132	
   133	---
   134	
   135	## 8. PLAN BEFORE ACTING
   136	
   137	Scope you have not thought through is scope you will discover mid-edit, which is where
   138	drift starts. Before the first change on any multi-step work, decide three things and
   139	say them:
   140	
   141	1. **Which files you will read first.** Reading first is `prevent-overengineering.md` §2's
   142	   pre-write pass, and it is not repeated here, but *which* files is a scope decision,
   143	   because the set you read is the set you will feel entitled to change.
   144	2. **Which tools or commands will do the work.** Naming them up front is what catches
   145	   the case where the plan needs something outside the frozen scope, while it is still
   146	   cheap to raise.
   147	3. **How the result will be verified.** `evidence-and-proof.md` §8 owns the proof plan;
   148	   what belongs here is that a change with no named check is a change with no defined
   149	   edge, and an undefined edge is where "while I was in there" gets in.
   150	
   151	Three sentences is a plan. The failure this prevents is not disorganization, it is the
   152	mid-task realization that the work needs a file nobody scoped, discovered at the moment
   153	when editing it feels easier than asking.
   154	
   155	---
   156	
   157	## 9. SELF-CHECK
   158	
   159	- [ ] Every file in my diff is in scope by §2, or has an explicit yes.
   160	- [ ] Adjacent defects are recorded and reported, not fixed.
   161	- [ ] Everything asked for is delivered, or listed as not delivered with a reason.
   162	- [ ] No formatting or cleanup noise rides along with the real change.
   163	- [ ] I named the files, the tools, and the check before the first edit.
   164	- [ ] Nothing entered the plan that was not in the frozen scope.
   165	- [ ] Every action on the always-needs-a-yes list had its yes before it happened.
   166	- [ ] Every deviation from the approved plan was stated and approved, and a plan I judged wrong was followed with the amendment proposed beside it.

