    28	---
    29	
    30	# Rule: Prevent overengineering
    31	
    32	> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.
    33	> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.
    34	
    35	## Fires when
    36	
    37	- Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.
    38	- Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.
    39	- Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.
    40	- Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.
    41	
    42	## The rule
    43	
    44	**Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only
    45	after naming what fails at the smaller one.**
    46	
    47	The naming is the whole rule. "It's cleaner" is not a failure. "A future caller might"
    48	is not a failure. A failure is something that breaks today, for a requirement that
    49	exists today.
    50	
    51	---
    52	
    53	## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER
    54	
    55	Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence
    56	that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.
    57	
    58	| Move | Cost when wrong |
    59	|------|-----------------|
    60	| **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |
    61	| Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |
    62	| Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |
    63	| Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |
    64	| Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |
    65	| Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |
    66	| Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |
    67	
    68	> **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered
    69	> rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the
    70	> authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them, because
    71	> where that file sits is each repository's own business. That ladder orders *solution
    72	> sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite
    73	> rung numbers from that file, and cite moves by name from this one. Naming a "rung 2"
    74	> here would mean something different there, which is exactly the confusion this section
    75	> stopped causing.
    76	
    77	Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied
    78	by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:
    79	
    80	> "Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with
    81	> incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function
    82	> beside it."
    83	
    84	If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching
    85	past the move you can justify.
    86	
    87	---
    88	
    89	## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS
    90	
    91	After reading the existing code, before the first edit:
    92	
    93	1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing
    94	   its climbing sentence.
    95	2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name
    96	   the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`), and the contract that must not
    97	   break. No real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code
    98	   should not exist either.
    99	
   100	---
   101	
   102	## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY
   103	
   104	Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.
   105	
   106	| Signal | What it means | Do this |
   107	|--------|---------------|---------|
   108	| A config option "so we can change it later" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |
   109	| A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |
   110	
   111	---
   112	
   113	## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS
   114	
   115	**Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an
   116	axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two
   117	real callers need different values today**.
   118	
   119	**Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting
   120	at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication, and duplication
   121	is cheaper to fix than the wrong seam.
   122	
   123	**Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or
   124	swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.
   125	
   126	**Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the
   127	layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next
   128	reader it *is* sometimes null, and they will code around a ghost.
   129	
   130	**Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder
   131	applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.
   132	
   133	**Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report
   134	baseline and delta, or leave it alone.
   135	
   136	**Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not
   137	actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline
   138	path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. "In
   139	case" is not an environment.
   140	
   141	**Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one is the costliest move in §1,
   142	needs its climbing sentence, and takes the `blast-radius.md` pass too, installing
   143	mutates the environment.
   144	
   145	---
   146	
   147	## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT
   148	
   149	Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.
   150	
   151	- **Not a license to under-deliver.** "This part is unnecessary" never authorizes
   152	  cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.
   153	- **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not
   154	  speculative.
   155	
   156	---
   157	
   158	## 6. SELF-CHECK
   159	
   160	- [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past "build nothing".
   161	- [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.
   162	- [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.
   163	- [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.
   164	- [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.
   165	- [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing.
   166	- [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.
   167	- [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.

