    11	  - "a finding is a hypothesis"
    12	  - "the sub-agent said complete"
    13	  - "the tool reported success"
    14	  - "claiming done"
    15	  - "final-state proof"
    16	  - "proof plan before implementation"
    17	  - "reason from data not memory"
    18	  - "the doc disagrees with the code"
    19	  - "honest close-out"
    20	  - "committed pushed dirty"
    21	  - "what only the operator can verify"
    22	  - "unconfirmed cause"
    23	  - "suspected cause"
    24	  - "cause then fix"
    25	importance_tier: important
    26	contextType: reference
    27	version: 1.1.1.0
    28	---
    29	
    30	# Rule: Evidence and proof
    31	
    32	> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.
    33	> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.
    34	
    35	## Fires when
    36	
    37	- About to say "done", "complete", "works", "fixed", "passing", or "no regressions".
    38	- About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.
    39	- A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.
    40	- About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.
    41	- You are closing out a turn.
    42	
    43	## The rule
    44	
    45	**A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim
    46	carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**
    47	
    48	---
    49	
    50	## 1. THREE TIERS
    51	
    52	| Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |
    53	|------|---------|--------------------|
    54	| **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |
    55	| **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |
    56	| **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |
    57	
    58	Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.
    59	That is how a confident summary certifies work nobody checked. What to do when a claim
    60	cannot be resolved is [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).
    61	
    62	**A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would
    63	have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim, whatever
    64	you ran. It belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.
    65	A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim
    66	is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read
    67	the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:
    68	derive it here, at the total's own level, because verifying its parts is not deriving the
    69	whole. A match that never distinguished roles: compare what the two sides are, not the
    70	digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the honest output is INFERRED plus the
    71	step that would confirm it.
    72	
    73	When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix
    74	order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, label it as suspected and name the next
    75	check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding. Against the tiers, a suspected cause
    76	is INFERRED, and the INFERRED tier already demands what would confirm it, so the clause
    77	adds a reporting shape and takes nothing from the proof. There is no unmarked case: a
    78	cause is either a finding with its receipt or suspected with its next check.
    79	
    80	The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next
    81	reader treats as settled and stops testing.
    82	
    83	---
    84	
    85	## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE
    86	
    87	**A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**
    88	Not launched. Not assumed. Read.
    89	
    90	- Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.
    91	- **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to
    92	  start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,
    93	  and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.
    94	- **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,
    95	  the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.
    96	- Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole
    97	  authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.
    98	
    99	---
   100	
   101	## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES
   102	
   103	1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed
   104	   before the first test body.
   105	2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink
   106	   that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing
   107	   is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a
   108	   source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all, and several files
   109	   here use NUL as a composite-key separator. Re-run with `-a` before concluding.
   110	3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a
   111	   metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.
   112	4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated
   113	   snapshot, an empty parametrization.
   114	
   115	---
   116	
   117	## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL
   118	
   119	**Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use
   120	to prove the fix.** Then the same check proves the change. A check that passed before
   121	your change and passes after it proves nothing about your change. If reproducing is
   122	unsafe, say so and name what you used instead.
   123	
   124	---
   125	
   126	## 5. BASELINES
   127	
   128	"No regressions" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.
   129	
   130	1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before
   131	   changing anything.
   132	2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.
   133	3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.
   134	
   135	Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and
   136	delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.
   137	
   138	---
   139	
   140	## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF
   141	
   142	Three task shapes fail in ways the checks above do not catch:
   143	
   144	- **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then
   145	  rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones
   146	  you missed, and a clean diff does not reveal an unprocessed input.
   147	- **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.
   148	  Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.
   149	- **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having
   150	  written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.
   151	
   152	---
   153	
   154	## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS
   155	
   156	A sub-agent's "COMPLETE", a reviewer's "P0", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each
   157	is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an
   158	unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody
   159	can explain. Confirming against the real symptom is
   160	[`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and what a delegate hands
   161	back is [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).
   162	
   163	---
   164	
   165	## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION
   166	
   167	For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the
   168	acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command
   169	or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.
   170	
   171	Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes
   172	the standard.
   173	
   174	---
   175	
   176	## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF
   177	
   178	Before any completion claim:
   179	
   180	- [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.
   181	- [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.
   182	- [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug
   183	      output, no unrelated file.
   184	- [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the
   185	      blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.
   186	
   187	---
   188	
   189	## 10. CLOSE-OUT
   190	
   191	Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. Four things, briefly:
   192	
   193	1. **What ran or was read, and what it returned**, with the receipts from §1.
   194	2. **What is inferred** rather than observed.
   195	3. **What only the operator can verify.**
   196	4. **The state of the work:** edited / committed / pushed / dirty, and which branch.
   197	   Four different states, routinely conflated.
   198	
   199	And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step
   200	was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is
   201	done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging, the hedging habit devalues the
   202	honest report when it matters. **The first report is the honest one:** a claim that
   203	becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was written, not when it
   204	was corrected. This section covers what happened. What is now the
   205	operator's to do is a separate report, see
   206	[`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.
   207	
   208	---
   209	
   210	## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY
   211	
   212	Everything above is about proving a claim after the fact. This is about where the claim
   213	came from. Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:
   214	
   215	**Reasoning from what the code probably does.** You have read this pattern a hundred
   216	times, so you know what `resolveConfig` returns. Open it. The cost of being wrong here
   217	is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way
   218	that looks like a different bug.
   219	
   220	**Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and
   221	a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the
   222	code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on
   223	*what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a
   224	defect.
   225	
   226	Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:
   227	does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it
   228	after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope. These are cheap before the edit
   229	and expensive after it, the same asymmetry as §8's proof plan, one step earlier.
   230	
   231	---
   232	
   233	## 12. SELF-CHECK
   234	
   235	- [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.
   236	- [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.
   237	- [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.
   238	- [ ] A before-number exists for every "no regressions" and performance claim.
   239	- [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.
   240	- [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.
   241	- [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.
   242	- [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.
   243	- [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.
   244	- [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.
   245	- [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.
   246	- [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.

