    27	version: 1.0.1.0
    28	---
    29	
    30	# Rule: Delegation and orchestration
    31	
    32	> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before work leaves your hands.
    33	> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.
    34	
    35	## Fires when
    36	
    37	- About to **decide whether** to hand work to another runtime at all.
    38	- About to hand work to another runtime: a CLI executor, a sub-agent, a fan-out lineage, a deep loop.
    39	- About to compose the prompt that a delegate will act on.
    40	- About to accept, quote, or act on what a delegate returned.
    41	- About to answer a judgment question, "is this the right design", "is this rule needed", "which approach is better", from your own reading alone.
    42	
    43	## The rule
    44	
    45	**When you hand work to another runtime you become its orchestrator, not its author:
    46	brief it with evidence and a frozen scope, and judge what comes back against the
    47	repository, no single model's verdict, the delegate's or your own, closes a question.**
    48	
    49	---
    50	
    51	## 1. THE POSTURE SWITCH
    52	
    53	One check comes first: **is delegating cheaper than doing it?** A dispatch costs
    54	a brief, a wait, and a verification pass. Work you could finish in the time it takes to
    55	write the brief is work you should finish, see [`prevent-overengineering.md`](prevent-overengineering.md).
    56	This is the first question, not a caveat at the end.
    57	
    58	The failure this prevents: dispatching as though the delegate were you with more time.
    59	It is not. It has no memory of this session, and no access to what you already ruled out.
    60	
    61	> **One lens, stated as such.** The claims in this file about how models behave under a
    62	> vague brief are judgment from limited observation, not measurement, the disclosure
    63	> §4 and §6 require of every judgment claim, applied here. What would change them:
    64	> a run where a deliberately underspecified brief returned findings as well-grounded as
    65	> a specified one.
    66	
    67	---
    68	
    69	## 2. BEFORE YOU DISPATCH
    70	
    71	Five things, in order. None is optional, and the first is a hard rule elsewhere.
    72	
    73	1. **Read the executor's own contract**, per `AGENTS.md` §10 Operational Mandates, the CLI dispatch bullet.
    74	2. **Bind the write authority.** Say where the delegate may write before it starts.
    75	   A delegate given the repository is a delegate that will eventually edit something
    76	   nobody reviewed.
    77	
    78	   **The freeze runs both ways.** While a fan-out lineage runs, the orchestrator
    79	   changes nothing in the repository outside that lineage's directory until the run
    80	   settles, because the runner's write containment attributes every out-of-lineage
    81	   change to the lineage, reverts it and fails the run.
    82	3. **Freeze the scope in the brief itself.** See [`scope-discipline.md`](scope-discipline.md).
    83	   Scope you did not write down is scope the delegate will infer, and it will infer wider.
    84	4. **State the shape of an acceptable answer.** Not the answer, the shape. A file list,
    85	   a table with named columns, a verdict plus citations. "Investigate X" returns an essay.
    86	5. **Pre-resolve every gate the delegate cannot ask you about.** A dispatched worker reads the
    87	   same gates you do and obeys them, including the ones that stop and wait for an operator.
    88	   Nobody is at its prompt, so it stops forever and reports success. Setting the environment
    89	   variable that waives a gate is not enough: it makes the waiver true, not observable, and a
    90	   model cannot read an environment variable. Put the answer in the prompt. Give the decision,
    91	   not permission to skip it.
    92	
    93	---
    94	
    95	## 3. A BRIEF CARRIES EVIDENCE, NOT PREFERENCE
    96	
    97	A brief that says *do what you think is best* gets back what the model thinks is best,
    98	which is a sample from a distribution, not a finding about this repository.
    99	
   100	**Put in the brief:** the files and symbols to read, with paths; what has already been
   101	ruled out and why; the constraint that must hold; the format of the return; the
   102	requirement to cite `file:line` for every claim.
   103	
   104	**Keep out of the brief:** your preferred conclusion. If you tell a delegate what you
   105	expect, you have built a machine for confirming it. Ask the question you actually have.
   106	
   107	The failure this prevents: a five-iteration research run that produces a fluent
   108	restatement of the brief's own assumptions, and reads as corroboration.
   109	
   110	---
   111	
   112	## 4. ONE MODEL IS ONE OPINION
   113	
   114	For a **factual** question (does this symbol exist, does this test pass, what does this
   115	file say), one delegate is enough, because the repository can settle it and you will
   116	check.
   117	
   118	For a **judgment** question (is this abstraction warranted, is this rule needed, which
   119	of two designs is better), one lens is not a finding. Do one of these, and say which:
   120	
   121	- **Diverge the lens:** ask a second model family, or the same question framed against
   122	  the opposite conclusion.
   123	- **Ground it:** convert the judgment into something the repository can answer. "Is this
   124	  rule needed" becomes "find the commits where its absence cost us something."
   125	- **Escalate it:** if it is genuinely a preference, it is the operator's, not the model's.
   126	
   127	Agreement between two runs of the same model is not corroboration. It is the same
   128	opinion twice.
   129	
   130	---
   131	
   132	## 5. WHAT COMES BACK IS UNVERIFIED
   133	
   134	A delegate reporting `COMPLETE` has reported a claim about itself. See
   135	[`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §7, a finding is a hypothesis until
   136	something you ran confirms it. Four checks before you quote a return:
   137	
   138	- **Does the citation resolve?** Open one of them. A fabricated `file:line` is the
   139	  cheapest thing to detect and the most expensive thing to propagate.
   140	- **Did it stay in scope?** Diff the paths it touched against the paths you authorized.
   141	- **Did it actually run?** An iteration count from a run's own summary is the run
   142	  describing itself. Read the state it wrote, not the story it told.
   143	- **Do not read the exit status as the verdict.** It has been wrong in both directions:
   144	  a dispatch that stopped at a gate and wrote nothing exits zero, and a dispatch that
   145	  wrote everything then hit a provider capacity limit exits one. Check that the artifacts
   146	  exist, then check their content. The return code tells you the process ended.
   147	
   148	The failure this prevents: a finding entering your close-out with the delegate's
   149	confidence attached and none of your verification.
   150	
   151	**When a check fails, there is a next step and it is not silence.** A return that fails
   152	verification is not discarded and not quoted. Re-dispatch with the brief corrected for
   153	what the failure revealed, or record the failure as the finding, a delegate that could
   154	not answer is evidence about the question. What you may not do is drop it, because the
   155	next reader cannot tell an unasked question from an unanswerable one.
   156	
   157	**Persist your side too.** The delegate's state is written down and yours usually is not.
   158	Keep the brief, the checks you ran and the verdict, in whatever artifact owns the work. A
   159	verified return with no record of the verification later reads as an unverified one.
   160	
   161	**When two delegates disagree, do not average them.** It means the question was
   162	underspecified or the evidence is thin. Find which, and say which. A tally is not a finding.
   163	
   164	---
   165	
   166	## 6. YOUR OWN OPINION IS ALSO ONE OPINION
   167	
   168	The rule cuts inward. Skipping delegation because you already know the answer is the
   169	same single-lens failure, minus the paper trail. When you are about to answer a
   170	judgment question from your own reading alone, either ground it in the repository or
   171	say plainly that it is your judgment and what would change it.
   172	
   173	The failure this prevents: shipping a confident design verdict that no evidence
   174	supports, in a session where checking would have cost one dispatch.
   175	
   176	---
   177	
   178	## 7. SCOPE TRAVELS WITH THE WORK
   179	
   180	The delegate inherits your frozen scope and cannot widen it. If it comes back having
   181	fixed something adjacent, that is not a bonus, it is an unreviewed change, and it goes
   182	back out or gets raised as an amendment. Widening scope is the operator's call, and
   183	delegating did not transfer that.
   184	
   185	You also stay accountable for what the delegate did. "The sub-agent wrote it" is not a
   186	defect report; it is the same sentence as "not my code", which
   187	[`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) already refuses.
   188	
   189	**Do not commit what you did not read, and staging by name is not enough.** A live
   190	delegate's edits sit in your working tree. `git add -A` is the obvious trap. The quieter
   191	one: **`git commit` commits the whole index, not the paths you just added.** A delegate
   192	staging its own work as it goes, which is the right instruction, stages into that same
   193	index, so your next commit takes its files under a message about something else.
   194	
   195	Name the paths at commit time, not only at add time: `git commit -- <paths>` or
   196	`git commit --only <paths>`. Two catches. A pathspec commit only sees tracked changes, so
   197	a new file still needs its `git add` and contributes nothing silently without one. And a
   198	pathspec built from `git diff --cached --name-only` is the whole index dressed as a list,
   199	so passing it sweeps everything exactly as passing nothing would. Build the pathspec from
   200	the paths you told the delegate to own, and filter the index against those, never the
   201	reverse. When a delegate's work does belong in your commit, read it first and say so.
   202	
   203	---
   204	
   205	## 8. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT
   206	
   207	- **Not a mandate to delegate.** A dispatch that costs more than doing the work is a
   208	  restraint failure, see [`prevent-overengineering.md`](prevent-overengineering.md). Most tasks are
   209	  cheaper done directly.
   210	- **Not permission to defer the verdict.** The orchestrator decides. A delegate that
   211	  returns three options has returned three options, not a decision.
   212	- **Not about human collaboration.** It addresses machine delegation. The parts that
   213	  generalize do so by accident, not by design.
   214	
   215	---
   216	
   217	## 9. SELF-CHECK
   218	
   219	- [ ] I read the executor's own contract before composing the prompt.
   220	- [ ] The brief names the files to read, the frozen scope, and the shape of the answer.
   221	- [ ] The brief does not contain the conclusion I expect.
   222	- [ ] Write authority was bound before the delegate started, not corrected after.
   223	- [ ] For every judgment claim I am about to repeat, I diverged the lens, grounded it, or escalated it.
   224	- [ ] I opened every citation I am about to repeat, not a sample, one resolved citation
   225	      proves nothing about the others, and §5 says why that matters.
   226	- [ ] The paths the delegate touched match the paths I authorized.
   227	- [ ] Where I answered from my own reading alone, I said so and said what would change it.
   228	- [ ] Delegating was cheaper than doing it, and I can say why.
   229	- [ ] A failed verification was re-dispatched or recorded, never dropped.
   230	- [ ] My brief, my checks, and my verdict are written down somewhere durable.
   231	- [ ] Every gate the delegate cannot ask about was answered in the prompt itself, not only waived in the environment.
   232	- [ ] I read the exit status as the process ending, and checked the artifacts and their content for the verdict.

